            <ArticleTitle>Total Elbow Arthroplasty for Distal Humerus Fractures.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>605-14</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.hcl.2015.06.008</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0749-0712(15)00074-8</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Total elbow arthroplasty is a good treatment alternative for selected patients with distal humerus fractures. Its attractiveness is related to several factors, including the possibility of performing the procedure; leaving the extensor mechanism intact; faster, easier rehabilitation compared with internal fixation; and overall good outcomes reported in terms of both pain relief and function. Implant failure leading to revision surgery does happen, and patients must comply with certain limitations to extend the longevity of their implant. Development of high-performance implants may allow expanding the indications of elbow arthroplasty for fractures.</AbstractText>
